 "understand": [
  {
   "p": "You learned to read <span class='ar'>الْ</span> in Stage 0. Now use it: it means <strong>“the”</strong>, and it replaces the tanwīn."
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "بَيْتٌ ← الْبَيْتُ",
     "en": "a house → the house"
    },
    {
     "ar": "قَلَمٌ ← الْقَلَمُ",
     "en": "a pen → the pen"
    },
    {
     "ar": "رَجُلٌ ← الرَّجُلُ",
     "en": "a man → the man (sun letter)"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "p": "Now put a <em>the</em>-noun first and a description after it:"
  },
  {
   "examples": [
    {
     "ar": "الْبَيْتُ كَبِيرٌ.",
     "en": "The house is big."
    },
    {
     "ar": "الْبَابُ مَفْتُوحٌ.",
     "en": "The door is open."
    },
    {
     "ar": "الْقَلَمُ مَكْسُورٌ.",
     "en": "The pen is broken."
    },
    {
     "ar": "الْمَسْجِدُ قَرِيبٌ.",
     "en": "The mosque is near."
    },
    {
     "ar": "الْجَبَلُ بَعِيدٌ.",
     "en": "The mountain is far."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "rule": "A <strong>nominal sentence</strong> has two parts: the <em>mubtadaʾ</em> (the topic, usually with <span class='ar'>الْ</span>) and the <em>khabar</em> (what you say about it, usually without <span class='ar'>الْ</span>). Both end in ḍamma, <span class='ar raf'>الْبَابُ</span> <span class='ar raf'>مَفْتُوحٌ</span>. This ending is called <em>rafʿ</em>, and you'll see why it matters in lesson 5."
  },
  {
   "tip": "Both kinds of sentence you know have no word for “is”: <span class='ar'>هَذَا بَيْتٌ</span> (this is a house) and <span class='ar'>الْبَيْتُ كَبِيرٌ</span> (the house is big). The second starts with a <em>the</em>-noun instead of هَذَا."
  }
 ],
